MEMO LAB-1: Literature-Sourced Protocols
Analogue Gravity in a Bathtub, and Fiber-Optic Wave Analogues

Statut: complement to MEMO LAB-0, based on a targeted literature review.

Main finding: an entire scientific field -- "analogue gravity" (Weinfurtner,
Nottingham; Unruh 1981; Steinhauer) -- does exactly what was asked: recreate,
in a water tank, a horizon, an ergoregion, a mechanism of no-return, with a
modest university-lab budget and published protocols. This is closer to P4
(bounce / no-collapse) and to the cascade than any experiment in the previous
memo, and the literature is directly reusable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Analogue gravity in a tank: what it is and why it is your experiment
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The physical principle, literature-verified. Unruh (1981) showed that a flow
whose speed locally exceeds the wave speed creates a horizon: beyond that
point, a wave propagating against the current can no longer travel back --
exactly the causal structure of a black-hole horizon, inside a fluid. The
Weinfurtner group (Nottingham) realizes this with a draining vortex -- water
escaping through the bottom of a tank while rotating, like a bathtub being
emptied -- creating a sonic horizon and an ergoregion, measured by laser
surface profilometry. A simpler, earlier variant (Rousseaux et al. 2008;
Weinfurtner et al. 2011): a flow channel, a streamlined obstacle creating a
region of high velocity, and an underwater speaker injecting waves against
the current -- the long upstream wave is blocked and converted into a short
wave, the classical-side signature of stimulated Hawking emission.

Why this is closer to the programme than diffraction or tube acoustics. This
is not merely an instance of P1 -- it is a direct instance of P4: the
mechanism by which a region of a wave system becomes inaccessible because a
flow speed exceeds a characteristic wave speed is structurally the same
mechanism that, in the program's formalism, produces the bounce below the
self-dual scale. The turbulent cascade in the dyadic model also shares direct
historical lineage with this field: both concern wave propagation in an
inhomogeneous flow.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Reusable low-tech protocol -- kitchen-bin draining vortex
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Materials: large transparent bin (~40x30 cm plastic storage box), a drain
hole at the center with an adjustable plug, an aquarium pump to recirculate
(close the loop and hold a stable water level), surface tracer powder (light
flour or glitter), a laser sheet (pointer + glass rod as in MANIP E from
LAB-0), a phone in slow motion filming from above.

Pre-registered prediction, simplified for a home budget: radial flow speed
increases toward the center (mass conservation, v(r) ~ 1/r to first order);
there must exist a radius r_h where v(r_h) equals the propagation speed of
shallow-water surface waves (c ~ sqrt(g*h)). Beyond r_h toward the center, a
wave packet launched from outside toward the center must no longer be able
to escape back out.

Observable without heavy instrumentation: perturb the surface with a drop or
a light tap at various distances from the center, film, and identify the
distance below which the ripple can no longer travel back against the flow
-- a direct, visible measurement of r_h, the horizon.

Mandatory negative control: stop the drain (no radial flow) -> no no-return
distance should appear; ripples must propagate freely in every direction.

What this experiment does not claim: it does not simulate any real
gravitational physics -- it is an equation-level analogy, as the literature
itself states plainly (see the cautionary reference below), not a gravity
experiment. What it genuinely instantiates is a wave-horizon mechanism in an
inhomogeneous flow, which is exactly the level of abstraction the programme
shares with this field.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Extension with an underwater speaker (closer still to Weinfurtner 2011)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Add a small waterproof transducer (or a speaker sealed in a plastic bag)
placed upstream of the obstacle/vortex, injecting a wave of known frequency
against the current. Prediction: below a critical frequency, the upstream
wave is blocked and reconverted; above it, it passes through. This
reproduces in miniature the long-to-short mode conversion setup of Rousseaux
et al. (2008) -- feasible with a longer channel rather than a simple
draining vortex (a length of PVC guttering cut lengthwise works).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Optical fiber: what the literature actually says is feasible at your scale
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

A recent review (arXiv:2512.15695, "An introduction to nonlinear fiber
optics and optical analogues to gravitational phenomena") confirms that the
horizon/Hawking analogy also exists in nonlinear fiber optics, but at an
equipment level (ultrashort pulses, controlled Kerr nonlinearity) out of
reach of a cellar lab. The reasonable version for you remains the one
already proposed in MANIP D+ of LAB-0: a closed POF fiber loop as a delay
line testing P5, without claiming the gravitational analogy. I do not
recommend pushing further on fiber for now -- the effort/result ratio tips
clearly toward the water tank.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. A warning from the literature itself -- cite this if you publish
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

A foundational philosophy-of-physics paper ("What we cannot learn from
analogue experiments", arXiv:1811.03859) is explicit: the source system
(water) and the target system (a real gravitational horizon) share an
equation, not a physics -- the analogy is mathematical (both obey a
Klein-Gordon-type equation on an effective metric), and licenses no
inference about real gravity. This is exactly the honesty clause the
programme already applies to T-duality. Any communication about this
experiment must carry the same caution: you are observing a wave-horizon
mechanism, not a black hole.

